 has been explained as an irregular fortition of -h-, which would be plausible if the form were of Upper German origin, where -h- was strong and where there was dialectal variation between /rx/ and /rk/. However, verkelen is first found in West Central German along the Rhine, where stem-internal -h- had been lost early on (Middle High German verlīn). It therefore seems at least equally probable that the -k- is due to influence by a related Rhenish word for “pig, piglet”, still found today in Ripuarian Ferke, which goes back to a Frankish *far(i)kīn (compare Old Dutch farkīn, ferkīn, whence modern Dutch varken), from Proto-Germanic *farhikīną, another diminutive of *farhaz.",{"g":"n","decl":"|||s|||n|"}]],"senses":[[4728,"ferkel","noun","piglet; a young/immature pig",0,"n"],[4729,"ferkel","noun","a dirty or contemptible person",1,"n",["derogatory"]]],"related_forms":[[7655,"ferkel","hirschferkel","derived"]]},"friaul":{"entries":[["friaul",null,null,null]],"senses":[[676028,"friaul","name","Friuli (A traditional region in northern Italy)",0]]},"geleitet":{"entries":[["geleitet",null,"/ɡəˈlaɪ̯tət/",null]],"senses":[[80473,"geleitet","verb","past participle of geleiten",0],[80474,"geleitet","verb","inflection of geleiten:",1],[80475,"geleitet","verb","third-person singular present",2],[80476,"geleitet","verb","second-person plural present",3],[80477,"geleitet","verb","plural imperative",4],[80478,"geleitet","verb","second-person plural subjunctive I",5],[80479,"geleitet","verb","past participle of leiten",6]],"inflections":[["geleitet","geleiten","present,singular,third-person"],["geleitet","leiten","participle,past"]]},"haarartigerer":{"entries":[["haarartigerer",null,null,null]],"senses":[[481762,"haarartigerer","adj","inflection of haarartig:",0],[481763,"haarartigerer","adj","strong/mixed nominative masculine singular comparative degree",1],[481764,"haarartigerer","adj","strong genitive/dative feminine singular comparative degree",2],[481765,"haarartigerer","adj","